#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124241 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124437 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "049e8586-8348-50be-ac56-4ea79eba43cd" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Slope of the Tangent Line to a Curve at a Point"}] :question/id #uuid "225b0bca-3520-58ab-bd36-0e3de337b604" :question/math-academy-id "e-3068" :question/problem "A surface $S$ is defined by $z=f(x,y).$ The curve $C$ is the intersection of $S$ with the plane $x=2.$ Given that the point $P(2,1,8)$ lies on $C,$ and that $f_y = \\dfrac{4x}y - 4x^2y,$ find the slope of the tangent to $C$ at $P.$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $x=2.$ So, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P(2,1,8)$ will be given by the partial derivative $f_y(2,1).$\n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative $f_y(x,y)$ at $(2,1)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(2,1) & =\\frac{4(2)}{(1)}-4(2)^{2}(1) \\\\  & =8-16 \\\\  & =-8\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124242 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124438 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4c5685f7-71b2-58ae-8c61-f555b355d92f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of the Tangent Line to a Curve at a Point"}] :question/id #uuid "70bcf628-cf1f-5da0-85b7-19d0f514ccd8" :question/math-academy-id "e-3069" :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $y=\\pi.$ Given that the point $P(1,\\pi,-1)$ lies on $C,$ and that $f_x = y\\cos(xy) + \\cos y,$ find the equations of the tangent line to $C$ at $P.$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z = f(x,y)$ with the plane $y=\\pi.$ So, the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point $P(1,\\pi,-1)$ will be given by the partial derivative $\\dfrac {\\partial z} {\\partial x} \\Bigg|_{(1,\\pi)}.$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative $\\dfrac {\\partial z} {\\partial x}$ at $(1,\\pi)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂z}{∂x}{|}_{(1,\\pi )} & =\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(1\\cdot \\pi )+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi  \\\\  & =\\pi (-1)+(-1) \\\\  & =-\\pi -1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-z{|}_{(1,\\pi )} & =\\frac{∂z}{∂x}{|}_{(1,\\pi )}(x-1) \\\\ z-(-1) & =(-\\pi -1)(x-1) \\\\ z+1 & =(-\\pi -1)x+\\pi +1 \\\\ z & =-(\\pi +1)x+\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation above represents a line in the $xz$-plane. The $xz$-plane through the point $P(1,\\pi,-1)$ has the equation $y=\\pi.$\n\nSo, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(1,\\pi,-1)$ is given by the intersection of the following planes:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=\\pi \\\\ z &=-(\\pi +1)x+\\pi\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124243 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124439 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b0325887-ce25-5d6f-bf27-d85541e1dbe0" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Tangent Vector to a Curve at a Point"}] :question/id #uuid "75c0deaf-38a7-5a81-afac-cae2978d52b4" :question/math-academy-id "e-3070" :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $y=1.$ Given that the point $P(0,1,1)$ lies on $C,$ and that $f_x =  \\dfrac{\\sqrt{2} \\pi}{4} \\sin{\\left(2 x + \\dfrac{\\pi y}{4} \\right)},$ find a tangent vector to $C$ at $P.$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface with the plane $y=1.$ So, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P$ will be given by the partial derivative $f_x(0,1).$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative $f_x(x,y)$ at $(0,1)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(0,1) & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}\\pi }{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2(0)+\\frac{\\pi (1)}{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}\\pi }{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-f(0,1) & ={f}_{x}(0,1)(x-0) \\\\ z-1 & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}x \\\\ z & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}x+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation above represents a line in the $xz$-plane. The $xz$-plane through the point $P(0,1,1)$ has the equation $y=1.$\n\nSo, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(0,1,1)$ is given by the intersection of the following planes:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=1 \\\\ z &=\\frac{\\pi }{4}x+1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTo find a tangent vector, we can find a normal vector to each plane and compute their cross product.\n\n- For the plane $y = 1,$ we write $0x + 1y + 0z = 1$ and find the normal vector $\\mathbf{n}_1 = \\langle 0,1,0 \\rangle.$\n\n- For the plane $z = \\dfrac{\\pi}{4} x +1,$ we write $-\\dfrac{\\pi}{4} x + 0y + 1z = 1$ and find the normal vector $\\mathbf{n}_2 = \\left\\langle -\\dfrac{\\pi}4,0,1 \\right\\rangle.$\n\nComputing the cross product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨0,1,0⟩\\times ⟨-\\frac{\\pi }{4},0,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -\\pi /4 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =i+\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt this point, we've found a tangent vector, and this could be our final answer. However, just to make our answer a little nicer, we can multiply the vector by $4$ to yield a simpler, parallel vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\cdot (i+\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\,k)=4\\,i+\\pi \\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268882 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124437 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "049e8586-8348-50be-ac56-4ea79eba43cd" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Slope of the Tangent Line to a Curve at a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "226ad3f6-7e7b-5847-9314-07159469d3e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{25}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266452} {:answer/id #uuid "34c500a5-2bfc-5d7d-af3c-b5668516b746" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{15}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266606} {:answer/id #uuid "97cf4b27-8cb2-5296-96fe-683228875813" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267503} {:answer/id #uuid "a5bb424a-1a21-5451-bf5c-bf751e9a4479" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{7}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267641} {:answer/id #uuid "a5ddca65-ba60-5a83-84d5-38e7eeab917a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{16}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267643}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "226ad3f6-7e7b-5847-9314-07159469d3e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{25}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266452} :answer-field/id #uuid "c9bb7d99-021d-5d65-952d-0bbc4117f169" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268768}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "09f5edfe-2ef7-5aa3-b9a3-3d13ae59dd49" :question/math-academy-id "q-132584" :question/problem "A surface $S$ is defined by $z=f(x,y).$ The curve $C$ is the intersection of $S$ with the plane $y=-3.$ Given that the point $P(1,-3,8)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{x}=\\frac{{x}^{2}+1}{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}+y\\sqrt{x}$, find the slope of the tangent to $C$ at $P.$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $y=-3.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P(1,-3,8)$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(1,-3).$\n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(x,y)$ at $(1,-3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(1,-3) & =\\frac{{1}^{2}+1}{{1}^{2}(-3)^{2}}+(-3)\\sqrt{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{9}-3(1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{25}{9}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268887 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124438 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4c5685f7-71b2-58ae-8c61-f555b355d92f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of the Tangent Line to a Curve at a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "234ff552-30db-5efc-99f4-6ca2b3ba1ac1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=2 \\\\ z &=4y-\\pi\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499732} {:answer/id #uuid "6b8f64a9-ff65-58b8-9065-4abeb8498b0d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=2 \\\\ z &=2y-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499859} {:answer/id #uuid "8405b123-1df2-58c7-a42b-133a4390e156" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=2 \\\\ z &=2y+\\pi\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499905} {:answer/id #uuid "89e9a579-0e60-5841-a0a0-a7fc520c336b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=2 \\\\ z &=4y-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499923} {:answer/id #uuid "9b5f83b9-acd0-55f1-b0be-802d7971c9b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=2 \\\\ z &=4y\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499954}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "234ff552-30db-5efc-99f4-6ca2b3ba1ac1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=2 \\\\ z &=4y-\\pi\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499732} :answer-field/id #uuid "f4e7d076-c660-5037-a4ff-af8a315c239c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268835}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "22a41e65-3d40-52de-a0eb-a4c7353c0c15" :question/math-academy-id "q-132593" :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $x=2.$ Given that the point $P(2,\\frac{\\pi }{4},0)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{y}=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)$, find the equations of the tangent line to $C$ at $P.$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $x=2.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point $P(2,\\frac{\\pi }{4},0)$ will be given by the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂y}{|}_{(2,\\pi /4)}.$\n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂y}$ at $(2,\\frac{\\pi }{4}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(2,\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =4\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =4\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-z{|}_{(2,\\pi /4)} & =\\frac{∂z}{∂y}{|}_{(2,\\pi /4)}(y-\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\ z-0 & =4(y-\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\ z & =4y-\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(2,\\frac{\\pi }{4},0)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=2 \\\\ z &=4y-\\pi\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124957 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124437 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "049e8586-8348-50be-ac56-4ea79eba43cd" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Slope of the Tangent Line to a Curve at a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3bf0df7e-022b-5418-9924-365a7162eff3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321611} {:answer/id #uuid "3fa3c59a-6624-5598-a1a5-f291f19779f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321648} {:answer/id #uuid "44d2ebf6-d34d-5c73-86a9-e87ea0a289de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321692} {:answer/id #uuid "9a6fc4ed-289d-56da-b773-3e27dbdc2b79" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322372} {:answer/id #uuid "bd68ef18-667b-55d2-a107-cbd29381e44b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322617}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "44d2ebf6-d34d-5c73-86a9-e87ea0a289de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321692} :answer-field/id #uuid "090efca4-d7f7-51f0-927e-9f82cbd619ce" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323185}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "63eca657-711b-57df-bd85-bce2f11b802d" :question/math-academy-id "q-36216" :question/problem "A surface $S$ is defined by $z=f(x,y).$ The curve $C$ is the intersection of $S$ with the plane $y=3.$ Given that the point $P(2,3,9)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{x}=6x-y$, find the slope of the tangent to $C$ at $P.$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $y=3.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P(2,3,9)$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(2,3).$\n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(x,y)$ at $(2,3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(2,3) & =6(2)-(3)=9\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124958 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124438 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4c5685f7-71b2-58ae-8c61-f555b355d92f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of the Tangent Line to a Curve at a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0315c355-94e8-5b85-bc0d-c26e707e74cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=1 \\\\ z &=\\frac{4}{5}y-\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321176} {:answer/id #uuid "9db2c19a-f9cc-5cd6-8a22-46dc603af896" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=1 \\\\ z &=\\frac{5}{4}y+\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322389} {:answer/id #uuid "bc186f0e-14b9-56f8-a702-fc4364be3a61" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=1 \\\\ z &=-\\frac{5}{4}y+\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322605} {:answer/id #uuid "e1c279f3-89f6-5f4b-b92c-736849d41a97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=2 \\\\ z &=-\\frac{5}{4}y+\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322911} {:answer/id #uuid "f880ea2c-47c4-55a4-afa7-5ab8c96b72f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=1 \\\\ z &=\\frac{5}{2}y-\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323101}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9db2c19a-f9cc-5cd6-8a22-46dc603af896" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=1 \\\\ z &=\\frac{5}{4}y+\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322389} :answer-field/id #uuid "36f4a259-af06-543c-939e-9833986d33ba" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323278}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "491a90b9-f33c-5937-8491-8e8447d7cf82" :question/math-academy-id "q-36219" :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $x=1.$ Given that the point $P(1,2,4)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{y}=\\frac{3x+y}{\\sqrt{6xy+{y}^{2}}}$, find the equations of the tangent line to $C$ at $P.$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $x=1.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point $P(1,2,4)$ will be given by the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂y}{|}_{(1,2)}.$\n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂y}$ at $(1,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(1,2) & =\\frac{3(1)+(2)}{\\sqrt{6(1)(2)+(2)^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{16}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-z{|}_{(1,2)} & =\\frac{∂z}{∂y}{|}_{(1,2)}(y-2) \\\\ z-4 & =\\frac{5}{4}(y-2) \\\\ z & =\\frac{5}{4}y+\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(1,2,4)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=1 \\\\ z &=\\frac{5}{4}y+\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323802 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124438 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4c5685f7-71b2-58ae-8c61-f555b355d92f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of the Tangent Line to a Curve at a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03a88646-da7b-5cc5-a6ca-e3694eb78578" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=0.5 \\\\ z &=4x+2\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321182} {:answer/id #uuid "31748a7a-7455-5dc9-aebc-92650c97c472" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=2 \\\\ z &=4x+2\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321526} {:answer/id #uuid "a14281d6-156f-5f48-beed-65acb59f3aec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=0.5 \\\\ z &=4y-12\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322421} {:answer/id #uuid "af9c34b4-7ca6-552a-a577-8a9187d131ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=0.5 \\\\ z &=-4y+12\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322525} {:answer/id #uuid "d0f4b2b7-738a-52b8-9ea5-229baa45ac6a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=0.5 \\\\ z &=4y-4\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322780}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d0f4b2b7-738a-52b8-9ea5-229baa45ac6a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=0.5 \\\\ z &=4y-4\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322780} :answer-field/id #uuid "41b9822b-8388-5077-b4c1-9ebc7d355fb6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323295}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "12d0b28e-9c11-5054-bcb2-feb86bd6fc4d" :question/math-academy-id "q-36441" :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $x=0.5.$ Given that the point $P(0.5,2,4)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{y}=y+2y\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)+\\frac{1}{x}$, find the equations of the tangent line to $C$ at $P.$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $x=0.5.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point $P(0.5,2,4)$ will be given by the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂y}{|}_{(0.5,2)}.$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂y}$ at $(0.5,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂z}{∂y}{|}_{(0.5,2)} & =2+2(2)\\operatorname{ln}⁡((0.5)(2))+\\frac{1}{0.5} \\\\  & =2+4\\operatorname{ln}⁡1+2 \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-z{|}_{(0.5,2)} & =\\frac{∂z}{∂y}{|}_{(0.5,2)}(y-2) \\\\ z-4 & =4(y-2) \\\\ z & =4y-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(0.5,2,4)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=0.5 \\\\ z &=4y-4\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335768 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124437 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "049e8586-8348-50be-ac56-4ea79eba43cd" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Slope of the Tangent Line to a Curve at a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c9a56e0-5972-5209-9d5c-ea16e82e995f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333100} {:answer/id #uuid "66686a01-21cc-56d7-951a-bacb93258c0f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333921} {:answer/id #uuid "79013707-fe29-522d-ac8d-ac18b46d1880" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334075} {:answer/id #uuid "989e0c9d-3b24-5671-9e42-b86c26764caf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334342} {:answer/id #uuid "b3886518-c45d-5987-8dfe-39a52ed04936" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334564}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "66686a01-21cc-56d7-951a-bacb93258c0f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333921} :answer-field/id #uuid "6a4e3654-a019-5e30-92b9-ed3bae770d8d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335446}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1f6c71da-f5b0-5c03-871d-e07fb324730c" :question/math-academy-id "q-64968" :question/problem "A surface $S$ is defined by $z=f(x,y).$ The curve $C$ is the intersection of $S$ with the plane $x=1.$ Given that the point $P$ with $y$-coordinate $0$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{y}=\\sqrt{2}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy+\\frac{\\pi }{4})$, find the slope of the tangent to $C$ at $P.$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $x=1.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point where $y=0$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(1,0).$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(x,y)$ at $(1,0):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(1,0) & =\\sqrt{2}(1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1\\cdot 0+\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125183 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124439 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b0325887-ce25-5d6f-bf27-d85541e1dbe0" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Tangent Vector to a Curve at a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "25cf98fa-a5b3-5e6b-b710-84fa1db64ec9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "j-3\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407304} {:answer/id #uuid "5de2add0-6423-5754-a07f-96730ef51cc3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-j-2\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407307} {:answer/id #uuid "bccf173f-8570-55d3-b189-673a90a1c377" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3j+k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407310} {:answer/id #uuid "db135884-f54c-58f1-b6d6-52b5e9137f78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2j+3\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407312} {:answer/id #uuid "fd44ef3e-d356-53fe-b14e-c4931ae0f4a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2j+\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407313}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "25cf98fa-a5b3-5e6b-b710-84fa1db64ec9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "j-3\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407304} :answer-field/id #uuid "a315569d-b4ce-597c-a11e-e51ae4013a54" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407314}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ecca0679-247f-506b-95a3-b2be27b80569" :question/math-academy-id "q-64972" :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $x=1.$ Given that the point $P(1,-1,2)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{y}={x}^{2}(2xy-1)$, which of the following gives a tangent vector to $C$ at $P?$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface with the plane $x=1.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(1,-1).$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(x,y)$ at $(1,-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(1,-1) & =(1)^{2}(2(1)(-1)-1)=-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-f(1,-1) & ={f}_{y}(1,-1)(y+1) \\\\ z-2 & =-3(y+1) \\\\ z-2 & =-3y-3 \\\\ z & =-3y-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(1,-1,2)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=1 \\\\ z &=-3y-1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNext, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $x=1,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨1,0,0⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $z=-3y-1$ (or $-3y-z-1=0$), a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨0,-3,-1⟩.$\n\nFinally, to find a direction vector for the tangent line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨1,0,0⟩\\times ⟨0,-3,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & -1\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =j-3\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a tangent vector to $C$ at $P$ is $j-3\\,k.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338912 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124437 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "049e8586-8348-50be-ac56-4ea79eba43cd" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Slope of the Tangent Line to a Curve at a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "34d5f7cf-613e-525d-93c2-0c7080720d91" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336530} {:answer/id #uuid "6b07d985-3564-5d0a-b070-9e5e048515e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336980} {:answer/id #uuid "c6298338-e8f0-5052-8bae-ef3ddf73ce7d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337789} {:answer/id #uuid "e6ad9eb0-f197-5640-819b-488c340e4e28" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338046} {:answer/id #uuid "f52f84db-f262-57d1-9c5e-5a630266362b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338178}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e6ad9eb0-f197-5640-819b-488c340e4e28" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338046} :answer-field/id #uuid "7598b8df-f496-507a-9973-2189f2324d0b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338478}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6efe9457-81ed-5961-99c6-f2de702a7e78" :question/math-academy-id "q-71198" :question/problem "A surface $S$ is defined by $z=f(x,y).$ The curve $C$ is the intersection of $S$ with the plane $x=2.$ Given that the point $P$ with $y$-coordinate $1$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{y}=3{x}^{2}y-6x{y}^{2}$, find the slope of the tangent to $C$ at $P.$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $x=2.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point where $y=1$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(2,1).$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(x,y)$ at $(2,1):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(2,1) & =3(2)^{2}(1)-6(2)(1)^{2} \\\\  & =12-12 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338917 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124438 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4c5685f7-71b2-58ae-8c61-f555b355d92f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of the Tangent Line to a Curve at a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "156f0e60-a655-5cee-ace2-00f94feeb9b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=1 \\\\ z &=x-2\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336234} {:answer/id #uuid "3998953b-84b0-5889-81f7-4dc1224db93d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=1 \\\\ z &=x+2\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336573} {:answer/id #uuid "4603346d-02b6-5575-9939-64549e4f33fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=1 \\\\ z &=2\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336681} {:answer/id #uuid "6b358259-0538-5cb3-840e-3b6005519fca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=1 \\\\ z &=-x+2\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336981} {:answer/id #uuid "ef59035a-1a91-53fe-8a72-a5d6bc5d3dc8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=1 \\\\ z &=-2\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338127}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3998953b-84b0-5889-81f7-4dc1224db93d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=1 \\\\ z &=x+2\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336573} :answer-field/id #uuid "67c2ebb0-7f04-5390-9b3f-6c5b9ac02ee1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338458}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ceda561f-8269-5197-b05a-efc41af9a9a2" :question/math-academy-id "q-71218" :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $y=1.$ Given that the point $P(0,1,2)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{x}={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)$, find the equations of the tangent line to $C$ at $P.$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $y=1.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point $P(0,1,2)$ will be given by the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂x}{|}_{(0,1)}.$\n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂x}$ at $(0,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂z}{∂x}{|}_{(0,1)} & =(0)^{2}+(1)^{2}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0+1) \\\\  & =1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡1 \\\\  & =1-0 \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-z{|}_{(0,1)} & =\\frac{∂z}{∂x}{|}_{(0,1)}(x-0) \\\\ z-2 & =1(x) \\\\ z & =x+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(0,1,2)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=1 \\\\ z &=x+2\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338918 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124439 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b0325887-ce25-5d6f-bf27-d85541e1dbe0" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Tangent Vector to a Curve at a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0284616a-7c89-5ca0-af71-91a32977c6fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,-1,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336053} {:answer/id #uuid "208d69f2-61c9-5cef-ae67-df4b82077458" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,1,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336339} {:answer/id #uuid "4733129a-0c72-56e1-9873-d6951efbe108" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,6,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336692} {:answer/id #uuid "54939340-4ca9-5a82-b24c-2542ed64af8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,-6,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336814} {:answer/id #uuid "fb04c736-1e93-5392-937a-e6b52b89f395" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,-2,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338234}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0284616a-7c89-5ca0-af71-91a32977c6fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,-1,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336053} :answer-field/id #uuid "4ee8ed97-cdf9-56b8-9f86-2a9258c8b74b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338414}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "2d6bbe53-bb73-5d24-b77f-7c5fef3fe5bc" :question/math-academy-id "q-71220" :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $x=-2.$ Given that the point $P(-2,-6,1)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{y}=\\frac{y-x}{\\sqrt{x(y+x)}}$, which of the following gives a tangent vector to $C$ at $P?$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface with the plane $x=-2.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(-2,-6).$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(x,y)$ at $(-2,-6):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(-2,-6) & =\\frac{-6-(-2)}{\\sqrt{(-2)(-6+(-2))}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-4}{\\sqrt{16}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{4} \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-f(-2,-6) & ={f}_{y}(-2,-6)(y-(-6)) \\\\ z-1 & =-1(y+6) \\\\ z-1 & =-y-6 \\\\ z & =-y-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(-2,-6,1)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=-2 \\\\ z &=-y-5\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNext, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $x=-2,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨1,0,0⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $z=-y-5$ (or $y+z+5=0$), a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨0,1,1⟩.$\n\nFinally, to find a direction vector for the tangent line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨1,0,0⟩\\times ⟨0,1,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =-j+k \\\\  & =⟨0,-1,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a tangent vector to $C$ at $P$ is $⟨0,-1,1⟩.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338919 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124439 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b0325887-ce25-5d6f-bf27-d85541e1dbe0" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Tangent Vector to a Curve at a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "11a4a9dc-c717-5035-ba3e-987961f9ee72" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨1,0,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336198} {:answer/id #uuid "3f159693-7272-539b-8d07-778a3ed4938c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-1,0,3⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336624} {:answer/id #uuid "596e8a9c-9c2e-5135-a08e-ad772219a349" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨1,0,3⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336849} {:answer/id #uuid "749562ba-1b02-5f83-a973-8bffa1c80cac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨1,0,-3⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337081} {:answer/id #uuid "de8038e5-bdf2-5d72-8003-0ca0304fe0a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨3,0,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337971}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "596e8a9c-9c2e-5135-a08e-ad772219a349" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨1,0,3⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336849} :answer-field/id #uuid "9f6508aa-26cc-5e67-a21e-e797c69eabff" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338568}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "8b7dd45b-b8d1-582b-9fbf-c50179d63d9b" :question/math-academy-id "q-71221" :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $y=1.$ Given that the point $P(-1,1,-1)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{x}=3{e}^{x+y}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|xy|$, which of the following gives a tangent vector to $C$ at $P?$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface with the plane $y=1.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(-1,1).$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(x,y)$ at $(-1,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(-1,1) & =3{e}^{-1+1}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|(-1)\\cdot 1| \\\\  & =3\\cdot 1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡1 \\\\  & =3-0 \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-f(-1,1) & ={f}_{x}(-1,1)(x-(-1)) \\\\ z-(-1) & =3(x+1) \\\\ z+1 & =3x+3 \\\\ z & =3x+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(-1,1,-1)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=1 \\\\ z &=3x+2\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNext, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $y=1,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨0,1,0⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $z=3x+2$ (or $3x-z+2=0$), a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨3,0,-1⟩.$\n\nFinally, to find a direction vector for the tangent line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨0,1,0⟩\\times ⟨3,0,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =-i-3\\,k \\\\  & =⟨-1,0,-3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $⟨-1,0,-3⟩$ is parallel to the tangent line, then $-⟨-1,0,-3⟩=⟨1,0,3⟩$ is also parallel to it."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425120 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124439 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b0325887-ce25-5d6f-bf27-d85541e1dbe0" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Tangent Vector to a Curve at a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0fabdc74-7580-5ecf-957d-3da51c5c3bd8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "i+k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425092} {:answer/id #uuid "1291615e-eaf6-5649-a612-304df178a8dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,i+k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425093} {:answer/id #uuid "23478b53-a88f-568a-858a-7b25f93a7066" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "i-2\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425095} {:answer/id #uuid "7733cd29-624f-51f2-ae97-a884afdbb38d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-i+k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425105} {:answer/id #uuid "e4854410-0525-5e63-82a6-354e8d687ef9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "i+2\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425112}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0fabdc74-7580-5ecf-957d-3da51c5c3bd8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "i+k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425092} :answer-field/id #uuid "472a8778-3d0c-5974-8ff7-bc1d5d8b12f2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425115}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d880e565-bd50-5672-98e1-45142968e59d" :question/math-academy-id "q-71226" :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $y=5.$ Given that the point $P(4,5,-2)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{x}=\\frac{\\sqrt{{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}}}{2x-y}$, which of the following gives a tangent vector to $C$ at $P?$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface with the plane $y=5.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(4,5).$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(x,y)$ at $(4,5):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(4,5) & =\\frac{\\sqrt{(5)^{2}-(4)^{2}}}{2(4)-(5)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{9}}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{3} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-f(4,5) & ={f}_{x}(4,5)(x-4) \\\\ z-(-2) & =1(x-4) \\\\ z+2 & =x-4 \\\\ z & =x-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(4,5,-2)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=5 \\\\ z &=x-6\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNext, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $y=5,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨0,1,0⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $z=x-6$ (or $x-z-6=0$), a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨1,0,-1⟩.$\n\nFinally, to find a direction vector for the tangent line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨0,1,0⟩\\times ⟨1,0,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =-i-k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $-i-k$ is parallel to the tangent line, then $-(-i-k)=i+k$ is also parallel to it."}]}
